-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Divergent Execution:** Classic DDA implementations suffer from divergent execution on GPUs due to branching for different quadrants and iteration directions. This method avoids this by unifying cases via axis permutation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Banding Artifacts:** Banding artifacts appear when using `stride > 1.0` for efficiency. This is handled by applying a jitter value in $[0,1]$ to the ray [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Frustum Clipping:** Complex frustum clipping is avoided by relying on the caller to ensure the ray origin lies within the view frustum [S1, unofficial].
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Branch Divergence:** Classic DDA implementations suffer from divergent execution on GPUs due to branching for different quadrants and iteration directions. This method avoids this by unifying cases via axis permutation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Banding Artifacts:** Banding artifacts appear when using `stride > 1.0` for efficiency. This is handled by applying a `jitter` value in $[0,1]$ to the ray [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Viewport Clipping:** The method implicitly clips to the viewport by structuring the test so that a zero value returned by `texelFetch` for an out-of-bounds depth-buffer sample terminates iteration [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Frustum Clipping:** Complex frustum clipping is avoided by assuming the caller ensures the ray origin lies within the view frustum, clipping only the far end against the near plane [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-The method is optimized for GPU execution, minimizing peak register count and using conditional assignment instead of branches [S1, unofficial]. The inner loop contains simple fused multiply-add (FMUL) instructions, which are well-suited to the GPU [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-Used in production for reflections in ripply water, achieving 2 ms at 1080p on GeForce 650M [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Scaling:**
-The method scales well with GPU hardware due to its branchless nature and efficient use of instruction cache [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Ensures sampling at a contiguous set of pixels with no oversampling; efficient on GPUs due to branchless execution; proven in production in recent major game titles [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Quality decreases with larger stride values; banding artifacts may require jitter to conceal [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-Use for screen-space ray tracing tasks such as reflections, especially when performance is critical and the hardware is a GPU [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-Not suitable for scenarios requiring high-quality sampling without jitter or when the ray origin cannot be guaranteed to be within the view frustum [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of steps (`maxSteps`) or the length of the ray segment in screen space, whichever is smaller. The inner loop consists of simple fused multiply-add (FMUL) instructions, which are well-suited to the GPU [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Constant space per thread, plus the depth buffer storage.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Used in production for reflections in ripply water, achieving 2 ms at 1080p on a GeForce 650M [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Ensures sampling at a contiguous set of pixels with no oversampling, addressing the inefficiency and missing intersections of linear 3D marching [S1, unofficial]. Efficient on modern GPU architectures due to minimized branching and register usage [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Quality decreases with larger `stride` values, requiring jitter to mitigate banding [S1, unofficial]. Limited to screen-space data (depth buffer), so it cannot see behind occluders or outside the current frame buffer [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When efficient screen-space reflections or similar effects are needed on modern GPUs, and the scene is primarily visible within the current frame buffer [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When global illumination or visibility beyond the current view is required, or when high-quality sampling without jitter artifacts is critical and performance allows for more expensive methods [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The underlying method of 2D iteration is about 20 years old, evolving from Bresenham's algorithm through DDA [S1, unofficial]. Recent games brought details for reflection tracing and GPU optimizations [S1, unofficial]. Wronski [2014a, 80][2014b] and Valient [2014b, 90][2014a] sketched algorithms in presentations [S1, unofficial]. The full implementation details were provided by McGuire and Mara [S1, unofficial].
